On Saturday, August 6, 2016 at 2:38:02 PM UTC-4, Bob Carpenter wrote:
> The only issue with users specifying wrong gradients is that
> they'll get lots of divergences and either live with the wrong
> answer or clog our mailing lists.  We do have checks that they've
> defined it properly, but they're slow.

I agree that is the cost, but it is a self-inflicted cost for people who do not even bother to ever run it with test_gradient = TRUE. Morally, I don't think that should keep us from implementing something that responsible people would find useful or perhaps essential in some cases.

> We could remove the requirement that functions are defined if
> they are declared, but then there would be Stan programs that
> translate to C++ but don't link.  I don't know how to catch the
> error at that point and do something reasonable for the user.

Linker errors don't bother me as much as wrong gradients that compile. If it doesn't link, it is pretty obvious what the problem is. It is only non-obvious for someone who meant to define the user-defined function in the Stan program but forgot.

Ben

